  "variableMeasured": [
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "mode",
      "value": "explore"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "The hypothesis concerns a pure mathematical/ML problem (neural network capacity vs constraint count), so no physical simulation tools apply. I will implement a controlled numerical experiment to measure N_c(w) for small feedforward networks on abstract constraints (e.g., polynomial or trigonometric constraints). Steps: (1) generate a family of w abstract constraints; (2) sweep hidden widths N_c for each w; (3) train and evaluate on held-out constraints to find the minimal width achieving zero violation; (4) fit N_c(w) ~ w^α to extract α and test sublinearity. This avoids any materials/chemistry mapping and stays within the required \"small feedforward network only\" boundary. The run.log from the prior run will be reviewed to ensure no residual state interferes."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML experiment)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The experiment will produce a table of (w, N_c(w), violation_rate). If N_c(w) grows sublinearly (α < 1), the support for low-dimensional constraint manifold will be indicated; if N_c(w) grows superlinearly or saturates, the constraint manifold is likely high-dimensional. Expected outcome: α ≈ 0.5–0.8 for smooth analytic constraints, suggesting efficient compressibility by networks."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "target_files"
    },
    {
